1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,920
How much faster does AI actually
make the people who write code?

2
00:00:05,433 --> 00:00:12,369
Ask around and the answers point in opposite directions,
from enormous multiples to slower than before.

3
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:20,524
So set the loudest answers aside, look at the studies that measured
programmers at work, and ask what each one actually measured.

4
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:33,181
On the Lex Fridman Podcast, DHH described "that magical 10X, 100X, in a few rare cases, 1000X
productivity boost", and said that to get it you have to work with the AI agents directly.

5
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:46,712
From the other side, a run of videos and posts repeats one trial's result, in the words of the channel Macro
Lens: when experienced developers were allowed AI tools, "they were 19% slower than when they weren't".

6
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:49,513
Start with ordinary workplaces.

7
00:00:49,533 --> 00:01:06,885
One set of field experiments followed almost five thousand software developers at three companies, in their regular jobs and partly
over years, and estimated that an AI code suggestion tool raised their productivity, measured by their output, by about 26 percent.

8
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:18,600
The authors note that this is substantially smaller than an earlier lab result, and suggest why: some
kinds of coding are less readily helped by the tool, and coding is only part of a developer's job.

9
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:31,896
A separate trial estimated a roughly 21 percent increase in development speed attributable to AI, but
that estimate missed the usual bar for statistical significance, so it is less certain than it sounds.

10
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,448
Then there is the trial
behind those slowdown claims.

11
00:01:35,467 --> 00:01:51,763
METR ran a randomized controlled trial with 16 experienced open-source developers on 246 tasks in mature
projects they had worked on for an average of 5 years, randomly allowing or disallowing AI tools task by task.

12
00:01:52,067 --> 00:02:01,187
The tools were the February to June 2025 frontier: mostly
the Cursor Pro editor with Claude 3.5 and 3.7 Sonnet.

13
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:05,616
With AI allowed, tasks took 19
percent longer to complete.

14
00:02:06,633 --> 00:02:21,417
The researchers' hypothesis for why AI helped less than in earlier trials: their projects averaged about 10
years old and more than 1,100,000 lines of code, compared with the more greenfield projects in those trials.

15
00:02:21,433 --> 00:02:23,401
But would newer tools change that?

16
00:02:23,433 --> 00:02:34,017
METR's own 2026 update says that, from talking to participants, it believes
developers are likely more sped up in early 2026 than in early 2025.

17
00:02:34,033 --> 00:02:47,449
Its new raw numbers lean the same way: tasks took an estimated 18 percent less time for returning
developers and 4 percent less for new recruits, but both ranges still stretch to a slowdown.

18
00:02:47,467 --> 00:03:00,859
And METR calls this only very weak evidence: more developers now decline to take part because they don't want
to work without AI, which likely pushes its speedup estimate down, so the ones left are not a fair sample.

19
00:03:01,367 --> 00:03:03,239
And the multiples from the start?

20
00:03:03,267 --> 00:03:20,235
The largest figures so far come from Anthropic's own report on its staff: employees self-reported that they now get a 50 percent productivity
gain from Claude on average, and merged pull requests per engineer per day rose 67 percent when its engineering org adopted Claude Code.

21
00:03:20,567 --> 00:03:30,239
A count of merged changes is not the same as time saved, but even taken
at face value, both figures are an order of magnitude below ten times.

22
00:03:30,767 --> 00:03:40,103
So, how much faster? In the workplace studies, with the tools they tested,
the measured gains were modest, nowhere near the multiples in the hype.

23
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:54,041
For experienced developers in big, mature projects they knew well, early-2025 tools made them
slower, and whether today's tools have reversed that, METR's own newer data is too weak to say.

24
00:03:54,067 --> 00:04:01,051
But that slowdown trial holds a stranger detail, and it
is about what the developers believed was happening.

25
00:04:01,567 --> 00:04:05,935
Take a second and make your own guess:
how much time does AI save you?

26
00:04:07,967 --> 00:04:15,671
Before starting, the METR developers forecast that
allowing AI would cut their completion time by 24 percent.

27
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:25,024
After the study, with the work behind them, they estimated it had cut their
time by 20 percent, in the same trial that measured them taking longer.

28
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:35,149
Outside experts did no better: economists predicted 39 percent
shorter completion times, and machine learning experts 38 percent.

29
00:04:35,667 --> 00:04:39,099
So people overestimate
the gain? Not always.

30
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:56,212
In an earlier randomized trial of Copilot, a lab task on a greenfield-style project, the measured productivity gain was 55.8
percent, yet participants in both the AI and the control group estimated 35 percent on average: they underestimated it.

31
00:04:56,733 --> 00:05:06,501
So the same kind of guess missed in opposite directions: too high for experienced
developers in big projects they knew well, too low on a fresh task in the lab.

32
00:05:06,533 --> 00:05:17,933
METR's later update adds that some developers now self-report very high speedups, and
repeats that, as its earlier study found, such estimates can be quite unreliable.

33
00:05:17,933 --> 00:05:24,869
So can developers tell? In both of these trials, not
reliably: their guesses missed by a wide margin.

34
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:32,572
Every number in this video belongs to particular
people, doing particular work, with particular tools.

35
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:44,060
So when someone tells you how much faster AI makes them, ask what they measured, and if you want to
know for yourself, time comparable tasks with the tool and without it rather than trusting the feeling.
